    83	## 1. Setting and notation
    84	
    85	These follow hypD §5.
    86	- A field is A = (m, f), with f = f̂_{N,m} + (x − m)R, f(m) = N, fixed odd d and B = L[1/2, a].
    87	- A relation is a coprime (a, b) in a box of size L^{O(1)} with a − bm and F(a, b) both B-smooth.
    88	- Its row consists of:
    89	  - rational parities q(a, b) ∈ F₂^{D_rat}: the sign and v_p(a − bm), p ≤ B;
    90	  - the "high" block z(a, b): degree-one ideal parities, real-embedding signs, LV characters, and the
    91	    |S|-parity column when f is non-monic.
    92	- R is the row space and W = {u : (u, 0) ∈ R}.
    93	- Λ/2 = {u : ∏p^{u_p} ∈ (Z/N)^{×2}}. Gen = (Λ/2)^⊥, spanned by the ω functionals w_i(p) = [(p/p_i) = −1].
    94	- U is the target space: Λ/2 restricted to a set of well-covered primes, or the span of the sampler
    95	  supports.
    96	- "Deficiency" means codim_U(W ∩ U).
    97	
    98	## 2. Lever A: Hypothesis RK
    99	
   100	### 2.1 Duality [proved; standard, stated in codex rkrank_1]
   101	- W^⊥ = proj_rat(R^⊥).
   102	- codim_U(W ∩ U) = dim res_U(W^⊥).
   103	- Proof: a rational functional w kills W iff it extends to a functional (w, y) on R. That is, w∘q lies in
   104	  the span of the coordinate functions of z on the relation set.
   105	
   106	So a **dual obstruction** is a pair (w, y) with w(q(a, b)) = y(z(a, b)) for every relation. RK bounds the
   107	dimension of the rational projections of the non-forced obstructions.
   108	
   109	Linear algebra also gives dim W = rank R − rank(z-block). Hence
   110	codim W = D_rat − rank R + rank(z-block). This identity is asserted in the code (`harness.level_record`).
   111	
   112	### 2.2 Raw versus genuine certificates [proved, existential; codex rkrank_1, checked by us]
   113	- Let E₀ be the combinations S whose algebraic parities and chosen character columns vanish.
   114	- Then ∏_S(a − bα) has even valuation at every prime, so it defines a class
   115	  ρ(S) ∈ H_K := {x ∈ K^× : all v_𝔭(x) even}/K^{×2}. H_K sits in the exact sequence
   116	  0 → O_K^×/O_K^{×2} → H_K → Cl(K)[2] → 0.
   117	- So h := dim H_K ≤ r₁ + r₂ + log₂ h_K = O_d(log N), since |disc f| ≤ N^{O(d)}.
   118	- Order and index bookkeeping adds O(log N) more.
   119	- Genuine certificates form ker ρ, which has codim ≤ h in E₀, so dim W_raw − dim W_true ≤ h.
   120	- **Consequence.** If the raw deficiency on U is ≤ r, then any t > r + h sampler rows supported in U give a
   121	  genuine dependency with nonzero sampler projection. That dependency is fruitful with probability
   122	  ≥ 1/(2κ) (hypD §5).
   123	- Efficient extraction of the genuine combination still needs LV §6 (characters). This is an
   124	  algorithmic, not an existential, requirement.
   125	
   126	### 2.3 Theorem U: uniform rank growth implies RK [proved]
   127	**Setup.** Z₁, Z₂, … are i.i.d. with law μ on F₂^s. G is the space of functionals vanishing μ-a.s. (the
   128	forced space). Δ_M = span(Z₁, …, Z_M)^⊥.
   129	
   130	**Hypothesis (Exp_T).** For every subspace V of functionals with V ∩ G = 0 and dim V = k > r,
   131	Pr_μ[Z ∉ V^⊥] ≥ k/T.
   132	
   133	**Theorem U.** Let τ_r = min{M : dim Δ_M − dim G ≤ r}. Then E τ_r ≤ T·Σ_{k=r+1}^{s} 1/k ≤ T(1 + ln s).
   134	- Hence Pr[dim Δ_M − dim G > r] ≤ T(1 + ln s)/M.
   135	- And codim_U(W ∩ U) ≤ dim res_U(proj_rat G) + r.
   136	
   137	*Proof.*
   138	1. G ⊆ Δ_M always. Write Δ_M = G ⊕ V_M with dim V_M = k_M.
   139	2. If Z_{M+1} ∉ V_M^⊥ then Z_{M+1} ∉ Δ_M^⊥, so dim Δ drops by exactly one.
   140	3. Given the past, this happens with probability ≥ k_M/T. So the time spent at level k is stochastically
   141	   dominated by a geometric variable of mean T/k.
   142	4. Sum over k, and apply Markov's inequality.
   143	5. Finally, W^⊥ = proj_rat(Δ). ∎
   144	
   145	*Remarks.*
   146	- For one field, the relation law is the kept law on the finite smooth set X = X_{m,f} (nfsfruit Prop R).
   147	  So (Exp_T) is a **deterministic expansion property of X**: for every V with V ∩ G = 0,
   148	  #{x ∈ X : x ∉ V^⊥} ≥ |X|·dim V/T.
   149	- The natural scale is T = D·L^{o(1)}. A prime p ≈ B occurs in a relation with probability ≈ u/B, so
   150	  rare-prime spaces have escape rate ≈ k·u/B. That is the coupon-collector regime, and it is why the
   151	  rate must be allowed to scale with k rather than be a constant p₀.
   152	- (Exp_T) is the GF(2) analogue of de Boer–Ducas–Fehr's "p-evenly distributed" sampling condition, used
   153	  by de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski (arXiv 2512.01588) for lattice generation under ERH. Their condition
   154	  asks for a *constant* escape probability from every proper sublattice. NFS rows cannot satisfy that,
   155	  because of rare primes. The k-dependent rate is what NFS rows can at best satisfy.
   156	
   157	### 2.4 Fourier form and the moment barrier [proved; codex rkrank_1, checked]
   158	- E 2^{s − rank R_M} = Σ_w ((1 + μ̂(w))/2)^M, where μ̂(w) = E(−1)^{w·Z}.
   159	- Also Pr[S_k = S′_k] = 2^{−s}Σ_w μ̂(w)^{2k}, where S_k, S′_k are independent sums of k rows.
   160	- A single row has support size J ≤ L^{O(1)} (the box size), so Pr[S_k = S′_k] ≥ J^{−k}.
   161	- Certifying a collision probability near 2^{−s+r} therefore needs k ≥ (s − r)log 2/log J = D/L^{o(1)}.
   162	- **(B-M):** pair-correlation or other fixed-order moment information about relations cannot by itself
   163	  certify near-full span. This is the RK analogue of hypD's Prop P.
   164	
   165	### 2.5 Prop C: the forced obstructions [proved]
   166	Call a pair (ψ₁, ψ₂) an **algebraic identity** if:
   167	- ψ₁ is a quadratic character on integers coprime to some q₁ (a Dirichlet character, of any conductor);
   168	- ψ₂ is a quadratic character of (O_K/𝔮)^× for some ideal 𝔮;
   169	- ψ₁(a − bm)ψ₂(a − bα) = 1 for **all** coprime (a, b) with both arguments coprime to the moduli.
   170	
   171	By Kummer theory and Chebotarev, every dual functional on the smooth groups is the restriction of such a
   172	pair, of enormous conductor. So "algebraic identity" is a property of the realisation, not of the
   173	functional. Prop C says which realisations hold identically.
   174	
   175	**Prop C.** Let C_d = 4(d+1)². In an algebraic identity, the local component at every prime
   176	ℓ ∤ 2·f_d·disc(f) with ℓ > C_d is trivial unless ℓ | N. At ℓ | N it is a power of the pair
   177	(χ_ℓ, χ_{𝔓_ℓ}), 𝔓_ℓ = (ℓ, α − m).
   178	- Consequently the identities span a space of dimension ≤ ω + O_d(#{ℓ ≤ C_d} + ω(f_d·disc f)) =
   179	  ω + O_d(log N).
   180	- There are no archimedean identities on all of Z²: as (a, b) ranges over Z², a/b crosses m and every
   181	  real root.
   182	
   183	*Proof.*
   184	1. **CRT.** The moduli factor over primes, and (a, b) mod ∏ℓ^{e_ℓ} ranges over a product of local sets.
   185	   Fixing all other local coordinates at (1, 0) (value 1) shows each local component is identically 1.
   186	2. **Local reduction.** For odd ℓ ∤ f_d·disc(f), quadratic characters of (Z/ℓ^e)^× and (O_K/𝔩^e)^×
   187	   factor through residue fields. The local component is (a, b) ↦ χ_ℓ(G(a, b)) with
   188	   G = (a − bm)^{ε₀}·∏_{𝔩|ℓ} N_𝔩(a − bα)^{ε_𝔩}. This uses (x/𝔩) = χ_ℓ(N_{k(𝔩)/F_ℓ}(x)). G is a binary form
   189	   of degree ≤ d + 1 over F_ℓ.
   190	3. **The irreducible factors.**
   191	   - N_𝔩(a − bα) is the homogenised irreducible factor of f mod ℓ belonging to 𝔩.
   192	   - These are pairwise coprime, since ℓ ∤ disc(f).
   193	   - They are coprime to a − bm unless some 𝔩 has degree one with root ≡ m (mod ℓ), i.e. ℓ | f(m) = N.
   194	4. **ℓ ∤ N.** G is then a product of distinct irreducible forms, not a constant times a square, unless all
   195	   exponents vanish. By Weil's bound, |Σ_{(a:b)∈P¹(F_ℓ)} χ_ℓ(G(a, b))| ≤ (deg G − 1)√ℓ + deg G. For
   196	   ℓ > C_d this sum is < ℓ + 1 − deg G. Hence χ_ℓ(G) = −1 at some point, contradicting the identity.
   197	5. **ℓ | N.** The same argument applied to G/(a − bm)² leaves only ε₀ = ε_{𝔓_ℓ} with all other ε = 0.
   198	6. The excluded primes (ℓ ≤ C_d, ℓ | 2f_d·disc f) contribute at most d + 2 local dimensions each. ∎
   199	
   200	**Box-geometry obstructions [proved].**
   201	- If a real embedding σ_j has sign(a − bσ_j(α)) constant on the box, its column is constant.
   202	- If, in addition, sign(a − bm) is constant (it is −1 when |a| < m and b ≥ 1), then e_sign plus that
   203	  column, or plus the |S|-parity column for non-monic f, lies in R^⊥. In that case W ⊆ {u_sign = 0}.
   204	- There are at most r₁ + 1 such obstructions.
   205	- They are harmless to Design V: use positive targets, or pair negative ones.
   206	- **Correction to hypD §5 ("archimedean root near m").** That root exists only if H ≳ m. Diagnostically,
   207	  for degree-9 LV-style fields with R-height m^η, a real root within m/4 of m occurred in 0/20 trials for
   208	  η ≤ 1 and 20/20 for η ≥ 1.5. This is consistent with the root displacement N/f′(m) ≈ m²/H.
   209	- In the monic toy no such obstruction arises unless a real-sign column is constant 1 (P4 in §5).
   210	
   211	**Reading.** Every non-forced obstruction is an *accident of the smooth set X*. Its realisations hold on a
   212	positive density of all pairs, yet fail on none of the density-L^{−c} smooth pairs. RK asserts that such
   213	accidents span only L^{o(1)} (or, by V_κ, ≤ D^{2/3−ε}) dimensions.
   214	
   215	### 2.6 Lemma S: the rational model satisfies uniform rank growth [proved; codex rkrank_1 blind, checked]
   216	**Lemma S.** Let n be uniform among the B-smooth integers in [1, x], and let V be a k-dimensional space of
   217	prime-parity functionals. Then
   218	  Pr[n ∉ V^⊥] ≥ k·Ψ(x/B, B)/((1 + log₂x)·Ψ(x, B)).
   219	For x = N^{c+o(1)} and B = L[1/2, a], this is ≥ k/(D·L^{o(1)}).
   220	
   221	*Proof.*
   222	1. Choose primes p₁, …, p_k ≤ B whose evaluation vectors ev(p_i) ∈ F₂^k form a basis. This is possible
   223	   because V is k-dimensional on the prime coordinates.
   224	2. For B-smooth n ≤ x/B, the k + 1 smooth integers n, p₁n, …, p_kn ≤ x have distinct evaluation vectors
   225	   ev(n), ev(n) + ev(p_i). At most one of them is 0.
   226	3. Each integer y ≤ x arises from at most 1 + ω(y) ≤ 1 + log₂x pairs (n, i).
   227	4. So #{y : ev(y) ≠ 0} ≥ k·Ψ(x/B, B)/(1 + log₂x).
   228	5. By Canfield–Erdős–Pomerance, Ψ(x/B, B)/Ψ(x, B) = B^{−1}·exp(o(u log u)) = B^{−1}L^{−o(1)}, and
   229	   D ≍ B/log B. ∎
   230	
   231	**Where it breaks for NFS [assessment, agreed with codex].** The switching n ↦ p_i·n is not an operation
   232	on relations of one field. There is no (a′, b′) in the box with a′ − b′m = p_i(a − bm), and F(a′, b′) would
   233	change uncontrollably; F(a, b) ≡ Nb^d (mod a − bm) couples the two sides. A *bounded-congestion switching
   234	on the joint kept law* would prove (Exp_T) via Theorem U. We know no such switching. Varying m changes the
   235	field, and all rows of one dependency must share the field.
   236	
   237	### 2.7 Barriers for proofs of RK [proved]
   238	- **(B-F) Field averaging is useless for union bounds.**
   239	  - There are ≤ N^{O(1)} fields, so for a fixed rational functional w, Pr_A[w ∈ W_A^⊥] is 0 or
   240	    ≥ N^{−O(1)}. A union bound over the 2^{D_rat} functionals (D_rat = L^{a}) therefore needs all but
   241	    2^r·N^{O(1)} of them to be obstructions for *no* field. That is a pointwise statement.
   242	  - Abstract example (codex rkrank_1): take a spread {E_A} of pairwise trivially intersecting
   243	    D/2-dimensional subspaces of F₂^D and draw field-A rows uniformly from E_A^⊥. Every fixed nonzero
   244	    functional is an obstruction in at most one field, yet every field has deficiency D/2.
   245	  - So RK must be proved per field, as an expansion property of X_{m,f} (§2.3).
   246	  - The same holds for "many independent fields": W = Σ_i W_i gives (ΣW_i)^⊥ = ∩W_i^⊥. With L^{o(1)}
   247	    fields this helps only under a uniform multiplicative contraction of the surviving dual per field.
   248	    Fixed-functional survival bounds would need Θ(D) fields.
   249	- **(B-M)** is in §2.4.
   250	- **(B-C) Coverage does not imply span.**
   251	  - Rows (e_i, e_i), with rational prime i paired with algebraic ideal i, cover every coordinate any
   252	    number of times, yet W = 0 (codex).
   253	  - So "well-covered support" in RK must mean a span statement, not an occurrence count.
   254	  - E1's P2 tests exactly this for the toy.
   255	- **(B-L) Random-exponent generation is not available at NFS size [assessment].**
   256	  - Hafner–McCurley, de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski and Dixon-with-random-exponents prove generation
   257	    because, given the observable, the relation vector is (quasi-)uniform on a coset of the relation
   258	    lattice.
   259	  - A single NFS row has support ≤ L^{O(1)}, far below 2^{Θ(D)} = |Λ/2|.
   260	  - We checked the obvious substitute, the twisted sampler c_e·x² mod N small with c_e a product of k
   261	    factor-base primes. Its relation vectors e + vec(z) are quasi-uniform on (weight-k vectors) ∩ coset.
   262	    They provably satisfy (Exp_T) with T = D·κ·2^ω, by the argument of Lemma S with p uniform in its square
   263	    class.
   264	  - But every such row costs a full sampler smoothness test. Spanning with them is Vallée's own algorithm,
   265	    c = √(2θ).
   266	
   267	### 2.8 Theorem V_κ: weaker RK suffices [proved implication; formula confirmed by codex rkrank_1]
   268	**Assumptions.** Design V's inputs: LV supply at fixed odd d, LV §6, and a fibre-symmetric sampler of
   269	exponent θ with cost L^{o(1)} per call. RK is weakened to: raw deficiency on U ≤ D^κ = L^{κa+o(1)}, with
   270	probability ≥ L^{−o(1)} over the field.
   271	
   272	**Algorithm.** Collect D^κ + h + 1 sampler rows; by §2.2 this suffices. With η = 3/(d+1), the costs are:
   273	- linear algebra: L^{2a};
   274	- LV rows: L^{a+η/(2a)};
   275	- sampler rows: L^{κa+θ/(2a)}.
   276	
   277	**Optimum.**
   278	- c(κ) = √(2·max{η, θ/(2−κ)}), at a = √(θ/(2(2−κ))) when the LV term does not bind.
   279	- κ = 0 recovers √θ (Theorem V). κ = 1 recovers Vallée/Dixon's √(2θ) with no rank hypothesis, because
   280	  D + 1 rows are always dependent.
   281	- **c < 1 iff κ < 2 − 2θ**, given η ≤ θ/(2−κ). For Vallée, θ = 2/3, this means κ < 2/3, with d ≥ 9 for
   282	  every κ ∈ [0, 1].
   283	
   284	## 3. Lever B: small-square samplers
   285	
   286	### 3.1 Prop Q: consequences of a better sampler [proved implications]
   287	**Definition.** A sampler of exponent θ:
   288	- outputs x with |x² mod N| ≤ N^{θ+o(1)};
   289	- has Pr[εx] ≥ Pr[x]/κ for all ε ∈ μ₂(Z/N), with κ = L^{o(1)};
   290	- outputs z = x² mod N that is B-smooth with probability ≥ ρ(log h/log B)·L^{−o(1)}.
   291	
   292	**Inputs.**
   293	- Fruitfulness ≥ 1/(2κ) per dependency containing a sampler row (hypD §5, pointwise bound).
   294	- Smoothness counting in square classes. Take products of two smooth numbers ≤ √h of the same class, with
   295	  Ω ≤ K = o(log L) so that representation multiplicities are 2^{2K} = L^{o(1)} (codex rkrank_2). The loss
   296	  is only L^{o(1)}.
   297	- Preprocessing by trial division to exp(√log N), which gives 2^ω = L^{o(1)}.
   298	- Smoothness of batches by Bernstein's batch algorithm, at L^{o(1)} amortised cost (codex rkrank_2). So no
   299	  HECM assumption is needed.
   300	
   301	**Costs with per-call cost L^{s}.**
   302	- Dixon-style: max(2a, a + s + θ/(2a)), so **c_D = s + √(s² + 2θ)**, which is < 1 iff **θ + s < 1/2**.
   303	  - Codex rkrank_2 wrote s + √(2θ). That is an upper bound only; corrected here.
   304	- Design V, conditional: max(2a, a + η/(2a), s + θ/(2a)), so **c_V = (s + √(s² + 4θ))/2**, which is < 1 iff
   305	  **θ + s < 1**. This needs η ≤ c_V²/2, i.e. d + 1 ≥ 6/(c_V²) at s = 0.
   306	- At s = 0 these give **√(2θ)** and **√θ**. So the hypD claim "θ_V < 2/3 gives √θ_V" is correct, under
   307	  RK, the supply hypothesis and d + 1 ≥ 6/θ.
   308	
   309	### 3.2 Why 2/3, and Theorem L (linearisation barrier) [proved for the stated model]
   310	**Mechanism (Vallée; reconstructed by us and codex rkrank_2).**
   311	- In the cell x = x₁ + t, (x₁ + t)² ≡ x₁² + 2x₁t + t².
   312	- Absorbing t² ≤ h into the error leaves a 2-dimensional affine lattice of determinant N in a T × h box
   313	  with T ≤ √h.
   314	- A cell is productive only if Th ≳ N, i.e. h ≳ N^{2/3}.
   315	- Vallée's Farey dissection handles the skewed cells. The lattice point counts are uniform exactly down
   316	  to this threshold (Vallée §5.1).
   317	
   318	**Lemma (rank collapse) [proved].** Setup:
   319	- w ∈ (Z/N)^r, and the box 𝔅 = ∏[−T_i, T_i] with T_i ≥ 1;
   320	- s_ij = w_iw_j mod N, centred;
   321	- q(t) = Σ s_ij t_it_j is the integer quadratic form;
   322	- hypothesis: |q(t)| ≤ h on 𝔅 and 3h² < N.
   323	
   324	Then, unless a gcd with N reveals a factor, there are v ∈ Z/N, σ ∈ Z \ {0} and a primitive u ∈ Z^r with
   325	w ≡ v·u, v² ≡ σ and q(t) = σ(u·t)². The distinct points x₁ + w·t form a subset of
   326	{x₁ + vτ : |τ| ≤ τ_max}, with |σ|τ_max² ≤ h. Hence there are at most 2√h + 1 of them.
   327	
   328	*Proof.*
   329	1. q(e_i) = s_ii and q(e_i + e_j) − s_ii − s_jj = 2s_ij, so |s_ij| ≤ 3h/2.
   330	2. The 2×2 minors s_iis_jj − s_ij² are ≡ 0 (mod N), because S ≡ ww^T. In absolute value they are < N, so
   331	   they vanish. Thus S = σuu^T over Q, with σ ∈ Z because u is primitive.
   332	3. Pick i with u_i, w_i units (otherwise gcd gives a factor, or w_i ≡ 0). Put v = w_i/u_i. Then
   333	   w_j ≡ σu_iu_j/w_i ≡ vu_j.
   334	4. τ_max = Σ|u_i|T_i is attained at t_i = sgn(u_i)T_i. ∎
   335	
   336	**Theorem L (oblivious-cell barrier) [proved].** Setup:
   337	- a sampler draws a cell centre x₁ from a law with density ≤ λ/N on Z/N, independent of N's arithmetic
   338	  beyond that bound;
   339	- its cell is an affine family with uniformly small integer quadratic part, as in the Lemma.
   340	
   341	Then the expected number of x with |x² mod N| ≤ h in the cell is ≤ λ·2^ω(2h+1)(2√h+1)/N. For h = N^θ,
   342	θ < 2/3, a sampler with L^{o(1)} distortion therefore needs N^{2/3−θ−o(1)} cells per output, whatever its
   343	per-cell enumeration cost.
   344	
   345	*Proof.*
   346	- The set {x : |x² mod N| ≤ h} has ≤ 2^ω(2h + 1) elements, because each residue has ≤ 2^ω square roots
   347	  for squarefree N.
   348	- Combine this with the Lemma and linearity of expectation over x₁. ∎
   349	- Codex rkrank_2 gives the special case of interval cells.
   350	
   351	*Scope.* The barrier does **not** cover:
   352	- arithmetic (non-oblivious) choice of productive cells;
   353	- curved regions solved exactly;
   354	- arbitrary lattices.
   355	No unconditional lower bound on θ can exist without a factoring lower bound: polynomial-time factoring
   356	gives θ = 0.
   357	
   358	**Coppersmith [proved failure of the natural construction].**
   359	- For F(t, z) = (x₁ + t)² − z mod N with |t| ≤ T and |z| ≤ h, take the triangular shift lattice
   360	  N^{m−j}t^iF^j, 0 ≤ i ≤ 2(m − j).
   361	- It has dimension (m+1)² and det = (NT)^A h^C, with A = m(m+1)(4m+5)/6 and C = m(m+1)(2m+1)/6.
   362	- The short-vector condition tends to **T²h < N** (our Jochemsz–May count and codex's exact count agree).
   363	- Productive cells need Th ≥ N^{1−o(1)}, so the two never overlap.
   364	- With z known, univariate Coppersmith applies, but z ranges over N^θ values.
   365	
   366	### 3.3 Other routes [assessment unless marked]
   367	- **Ratio forms** x² ≡ u/v with |u|, |v| ≤ N^{θ/2}: these are equivalent to (vx)² ≡ uv, so there is no gain.
   368	  [proved]
   369	- **Class groups (LP, Seysen, Hafner–McCurley).**
   370	  - They behave like θ = 1/2 objects: reduced forms have leading coefficient ≤ √(|Δ|/3). That explains
   371	    c = 1.
   372	  - [proved, codex rkrank_2] Coverage obstruction: ideals of norm ≤ |Δ|^θ cover only O(A log A) of the
   373	    |Δ|^{1/2+o(1)} classes. So uniform class sampling cannot have θ < 1/2.
   374	- **Elkies (ANTS 2000)** gives points near curves via a compact cover. Tangent subdivision reproduces the
   375	  curvature threshold, so it supplies no sampler.
   376	- **Schnorr's SVP factoring (ePrint 2021/933)**: no rigorous smoothness, rank or fibre symmetry.
   377	  **Boneh's CRT decoding**: no square structure. **Hittmeir/Harvey**: exponential.
   378	- **Square identities.** A polynomial family x = P(t) with P(t)² ≡ Q(t)² identically would, if
   379	  fibre-symmetric, factor N directly (codex). So an easy identity cannot hide the hard part.
   380	
   381	### 3.4 Literature (codex rkrank_2, our literature subagent, and the critic in §7)
   382	- **Vallée.**
   383	  - Math. Comp. 56 (1991) 823–849; STOC 1989. The target interval is [−4N^{2/3}, 4N^{2/3}], with a Farey
   384	    order of about N^{1/3}/4.
   385	  - We did not read the primary PDF; AMS blocked it. Codex rkrank_2 and hypD_2 read it via ResearchGate.
   386	  - Global quasi-uniformity constants: unverified by us.
   387	- **Coron**, CRYPTO 2002 (LNCS 2442, 613–626): wider intervals, N^{2/3+ε}, statistically close to uniform.
   388	- **Gentry**, CRYPTO 2004 (LNCS 3152, 179–200, §4): exactly uniform sampling at width 8N^{2/3} in expected
   389	  polynomial time.
   390	- **No θ < 2/3 found** (16 OpenAlex citers of the 1991 paper checked by the literature subagent).
   391	- **Stange 2022/23 (arXiv 2211.06821v2).**
   392	  - Hyp. 3.1 is saturation of one coordinate line of an *integer* relation lattice; it is heuristic.
   393	  - Thm 3.2 is proved relative to a relation oracle; the output is always a multiple of the order.
   394	  - NFS is mentioned only as a remark. There is no GF(2) statement.
   395	  - Her "1 − 1/ζ(c+1)" should read 1/ζ(c+1) (codex; a typo).
   396	- **Rigorous rank results.**
   397	  - Balister–Bollobás–Morris (Annals 2018) give the first-dependency threshold for uniform integers only.
   398	  - Coja-Oghlan et al. give rank results for configuration models, not NFS rows.
   399	  - We found no saturation theorem for sieve relations.
   400	- **Rigorous c < 1:** none found. We checked the 115 OpenAlex citers of LP 1992 and the citers of LV.
   401	
   402	## 4. What would close the gap
   403	1. **(Exp_T) for one field's smooth set**, with T = D·L^{o(1)}. Equivalently, a *joint* switching lemma for
   404	   the NFS kept law with bounded congestion.
   405	   - By Theorem V_κ, it suffices that the non-forced dual has dimension ≤ D^{2/3−ε}, i.e. an expansion
   406	     statement only for V of dimension > D^{2/3−ε}. Large V are the "most generic" ones.
   407	   - A possible lever, not worked out: rows from L^{o(1)} fields *sharing m* but with independent R.
   408	     These have common rational parts, which allows switching (a, b) between fields. But dependencies
   409	     must be per field (§2.7).
   410	2. **A non-oblivious sampler below 2/3.** It would have to choose productive cells arithmetically while
   411	   keeping fibre symmetry. Theorem L shows that oblivious choices cannot work.
   412	
   413	## 5. Experiment E1 (pre-registered; exact; toy)
   414	- Commands are in `notes/rkrank_prereg.md`. Analysis:
   415	  `.venv/bin/python -m src.rkrank.analyze experiments/raw/rkrank_e1.jsonl`.
   416	- 20 instances (blum/tri × 64/80 bits × seeds 0–4), 72 level-records. Runtime: 15–40 s at 64 bits,
   417	  5–13 min at 80 bits.
   418	- 0 accidental gcd events.
   419	
   420	**Disclosed limitation (not a deviation).** The registered boxes were exhausted before 3.0·cols in 16 of
   421	the 20 instances; the analysis skips unreached levels, as registered.
   422	- Levels reached, per instance: 3.0 in 4, 2.0 in 8, 1.5 in 4, 1.25 in 4.
   423	- So the "levels ≥ 2.0" predictions rest on 16 records rather than the intended ~40.
   424	- 9 of the 10 80-bit instances stopped at b = 12000.
   425	
   426	| prediction | registered | result |
   427	|---|---|---|
   428	| P0 W ⊆ Λ/2 | all records | 72/72 — **holds** |
   429	| P1a excess_peel ≤ 4 (levels ≥ 1.5) | all records | 30/32 — **fails** (two records with 5: tri 64 s1 and tri 80 s1, both at level 1.5) |
   430	| P1b excess_peel = 0 (levels ≥ 2.0) | ≥ 80% | 12/16 = 75% — **fails narrowly** (the others have 1, 1, 2 at 2.0 and 1 at 3.0) |
   431	| P2a c₃ = 0 (levels ≥ 1.5); c₃ ≤ 2 | ≥ 90%; all | 32/32 — **holds** (c₃ = 0 in every record at every level except four at 1.0) |
   432	| P2b c₁ = 0 (levels ≥ 2.0) | ≥ 80% | 12/16 = 75% — **fails narrowly** |
   433	| P3 small squares supported on P₃ in W | ≥ 0.95 | 1141/1141 — **holds**; negative z: 514/514 |
   434	| P4 sign obstruction ⟺ constant-1 real column | all records | 72/72 — **holds** (no sign obstruction arose; every constant real column was constant 0) |
   435	| P5 δ ∈ [0.45, 0.55] (3 Liouville-type + 10 dense) | all instances | 20/20 — **holds** |
   436	
   437	**Descriptive, not registered.**
   438	- c₂ = 0 in 12/16 records at level 1.5, 11/12 at 2.0 and 4/4 at 3.0. Every residual obstruction therefore
   439	  involves primes occurring at most twice (c₃ = 0 everywhere at ≥ 1.25), and most involve primes occurring
   440	  at most once.
   441	- The excess over the peeling predictor shrinks with M:
   442	  - level 1.0: 4–23;
   443	  - level 1.5: 0–5;
   444	  - level 2.0: 0–2;
   445	  - level 3.0: 0–1.
   446	- c₁ equals dim W^⊥ − ω − #{occ = 0} in every record.
   447	- Small-square vectors *not* supported on P₃ lie in W at rate 416/473 = 0.88. The failures contain a
   448	  rarely covered prime.
   449	- Single-prime functionals have δ between 0.0002 and 0.10; dense functionals stay at 0.5.
   450	
   451	**Reading [experimentally supported, toy only].**
   452	- In these monic cubic toys the dual obstructions are exactly:
   453	  - the ω genuine characters;
   454	  - functionals supported on primes that occur at most twice, almost all accounted for by primes missing
   455	    from the algebraic 2-core.
   456	- There are no dense near-obstructions (P5), no archimedean obstruction (P4) and no fake obstruction on
   457	  well-covered primes (P2a, P3).
   458	- The two sharp predictions that failed (P1, P2b) failed because a handful (≤ 5) of rare-prime
   459	  obstructions escape the simple peeling predictor and the occ ≥ 1 cut. They did not fail through any
   460	  dense or "fake" obstruction.
   461	- This is the strong, sparse form of RK, "W = Λ/2 on the well-covered support", at toy scale. It says
   462	  nothing about the asymptotic regime: d ≥ 9, L[1/2]-size factor bases, the LV coefficient law.
