**RK remains unproved.** I obtain an elementary rank-growth theorem for the rational smooth-number model, an \(O(\log N)\) bound on the loss from raw membership to genuine certificates, and a verification of the proposed cost formula with its supply condition. The unresolved step is uniform expansion for the **joint kept law**.

Labels: **P** = proved; **C** = conditional; **H** = heuristic or literature assessment; **F** = false as stated. All square classes below concern units modulo \(N\), with sign included. Perfect powers should first be removed for the fruitfulness argument.

**[P] Duality gives a precise formulation.** Write a row as \((q,z)\), combining algebraic and character coordinates into \(z\). Then
\[
W^\perp=\operatorname{proj}_{\rm rat}(R^\perp).
\]
Indeed, a functional on the rational coordinates vanishes on \(R\cap(Q\times0)\) exactly when its restriction to \(R\) factors through the second projection. More generally,
\[
\boxed{\operatorname{codim}_U(W\cap U)
=\dim\operatorname{res}_U(W^\perp).}
\]
Thus RK asks for a bound on the dimension of projected dual obstructions, rather than on the entire matrix corank.

**[P] Genuine certificates require a slightly larger algebraic space, but only a small correction.** Let \(E_0\) be the coefficient space of combinations with vanishing algebraic valuations and chosen character columns. There is a linear residual-square-class map
\[
\rho:E_0\longrightarrow H_K.
\]
For complete ideal valuations,
\[
0\longrightarrow
\mathcal O_K^\times/\mathcal O_K^{\times2}
\longrightarrow H_K
\longrightarrow \mathrm{Cl}(K)[2]\longrightarrow0.
\]
For fixed degree and coefficient height \(N^{O(1)}\),
\[
h:=\dim H_K=O(\log N).
\]
Order, denominator, and omitted bad-prime corrections also have \(O(\log N)\) dimension when properly included. Consequently,
\[
\dim W_{\rm raw}-\dim W_{\rm true}\le h.
\]
This is an existential statement; efficient certificate extraction remains a separate algorithmic requirement. It agrees with the obstruction space bounded in [LV §6](https://arxiv.org/html/1805.08873v1).

**[P] This also resolves the existential “preserve a Vallée projection” issue.** If raw deficiency is \(r\), then \(t\) appended target rows give a raw dependency space whose target projection has dimension at least \(t-r\). Imposing \(\rho=0\) reduces that dimension by at most \(h\). Hence
\[
t>r+h
\]
forces a genuine dependency with nonzero target projection. LV postprocessing must implement this preservation efficiently, but there is no additional existential rank hypothesis.

The forced obstructions can now be distinguished carefully:

- **[P] Characters at factors of \(N\).** For each distinct prime \(\ell\mid N\), reduction \(\alpha\mapsto m\) gives
  \[
  \chi_\ell(a-bm)=\chi_{\mathfrak P_\ell}(a-b\alpha),
  \]
  away from normalization/index exceptions, handled by localization and gcd checks. On genuine certificates these force the rational Legendre characters. Their restricted rank is **at most** \(\omega(N)\), not necessarily exactly \(\omega(N)\). CRT and the odd-prime-power square criterion show that their common kernel is precisely \(\Lambda/2\). They therefore vanish on either proposed \(U\).

- **[P, with a geometric condition] Archimedean characters.** If a real root \(\xi\) and \(m\) lie on the same side of every admitted slope \(a/b\), then
  \[
  \operatorname{sgn}(a-b\xi)=\operatorname{sgn}(a-bm).
  \]
  This produces a sign obstruction for genuine squares. There are at most \(d\) real-sign constraints. **[C]** The existence and position of a root “near \(m\)” require an explicit coefficient-height argument; odd degree alone does not establish that location.

- **[P] Leading coefficient and cardinality.** Nonmonic normalization introduces a fixed square class raised to the number of selected rows. A \(|S|\bmod2\) column handles this, costing at most one dimension. Bad/projective/index bookkeeping contributes only the preceding \(O(\log N)\) correction.

- **[P] Uncovered coordinates.** An identically zero rational column gives an exact obstruction. A coordinate occurring with probability \(q_p\) is missed by \(M\) independent samples with probability \((1-q_p)^M\). **[F]** Three occurrences, or any fixed coverage threshold, imply saturation: coverage does not exclude duplicate columns, small components, or correlated blocks.

- **[P] Units, \(\mathrm{Cl}(K)[2]\), and Schirokauer-type maps.** Their square-class contribution is included in \(H_K\); fixed degree prevents a macroscopic loss from these alone. Class-group characters can also give purely algebraic dual identities, which disappear under rational projection. Small-conductor Dirichlet/Hecke characters are possible structured dual tests, but an equality between their rational and algebraic evaluations on the kept set needs proof.

**[H]** “Genus characters + archimedean constraints + support on rare primes” is a reasonable heuristic after full bookkeeping and removal of poorly connected coordinates. It is stronger than RK and remains unproved even for the whole smooth set \(X_{m,f}\). Arbitrary algebraic parity functionals cannot presently be classified as small-conductor characters.

**[C] Route (i): uniform rank growth is sufficient.** In a specified allowable \(s\)-dimensional row space, suppose every surviving dual subspace \(V\), of dimension \(k>r\), satisfies
\[
\Pr_\mu[Z\notin V^\perp]\ge \frac{k}{Dq},
\qquad q=L^{o(1)}.
\]
Each escape increases rank. Summing geometric waiting times gives
\[
\mathbb E T_r
\le Dq\sum_{k=r+1}^{s}\frac1k
=D\,L^{o(1)}.
\]
Markov’s inequality supplies constant success probability.

**[H]** No known estimate supplies this for NFS’s joint kept law. The minimal sufficient addition is the corresponding escape bound for the *adaptively surviving* dual spaces, on fields obtainable at the asserted relation-supply cost. A field-averaged bound for each predetermined functional does not provide that conditional statement.

**[P] Route (ii): Fourier analysis gives exact reductions, not automatic cancellation.** For a row law \(\mu\) on \(\mathbf F_2^s\), put
\[
\widehat\mu(w)=\mathbb E(-1)^{w\cdot Z}.
\]
For \(M\) independent rows,
\[
\mathbb E\,2^{s-\operatorname{rank}R_M}
=\sum_w\left(\frac{1+\widehat\mu(w)}2\right)^M.
\]
A bound \(2^{r+O(1)}\) for this sum would imply small corank with constant probability. Parseval also gives
\[
\Pr[S_k=S_k']
=2^{-s}\sum_w\widehat\mu(w)^{2k},
\]
where \(S_k,S_k'\) are independent sums of \(k\) rows.

**[P]** Small fixed moments cannot generally prove near-full span. If one row has support of size \(J\), then
\[
\Pr[S_k=S_k']\ge J^{-k}.
\]
Reaching collision scale \(2^{-s+r}\) requires
\[
k\ge\frac{(s-r)\log2}{\log J}.
\]
Here \(J\le L^{O(1)}\) and \(s\asymp D\), so this route needs moments of order \(D/L^{o(1)}\). Pair-correlation estimates alone are insufficient. **[C]** The extra hypothesis is an aggregate bound on near-obstructions strong enough for the displayed Fourier sum; a sufficient full-matrix version is stronger than the required projected statement.

**[P] Route (iii): the rational side admits the desired escape estimate directly.** Let \(n\) be uniform among positive \(B\)-smooth integers at most \(x\), and let \(V\) be a \(k\)-dimensional space of prime-parity functionals. Choose primes \(p_1,\ldots,p_k\le B\) whose evaluation vectors form a basis of \(V^*\).

For every smooth \(n\le x/B\), the \(k+1\) integers
\[
n,\ p_1n,\ldots,p_kn
\]
have distinct evaluation vectors in \(V^*\). At most one annihilates \(V\). Every output integer has at most \(1+\log_2x\) preimages in this construction. Therefore
\[
\boxed{\Pr[n\notin V^\perp]\ge
\frac{k\,\Psi(x/B,B)}
{(1+\log_2x)\Psi(x,B)}.}
\]
For \(x=N^{c+o(1)}\), fixed \(c>0\), and \(B=L[1/2,a]\), smooth-number estimates turn this into
\[
\Pr[n\notin V^\perp]\ge \frac{k}{D\,L^{o(1)}}.
\]

**[P]** Under the independent geometric valuation model with parameter \(\sigma\), a parity character has exact mean
\[
\prod_{w(p)=1}\frac{1-p^{-\sigma}}{1+p^{-\sigma}}.
\]
Friable Turán–Kubilius theory controls additive fluctuations; parity cancellation requires characteristic-function information beyond a variance bound. See [de la Bretèche–Tenenbaum](https://tenenb.perso.math.cnrs.fr/PPP/TKfriable.pdf).

**[H]** The joint constraint breaks the elementary proof precisely at its multiplication moves. Replacing \(a-bm\) by \(p(a-bm)\) need not preserve the primitive pair box, algebraic smoothness, or the algebraic functional. Even the rational marginal for one field is weighted by these restrictions. Moreover,
\[
F(a,b)\equiv Nb^d\pmod{a-bm}
\]
couples the two sides. **[C]** A bounded-congestion switching argument preserving the joint kept law, or an equivalent uniform weighted character estimate, would repair this route.

**[P] Route (iv): random exponents explain existing generation proofs.** In class-group algorithms, one samples a large exponent vector \(z\), reduces its represented class, and subtracts the factorization of the reduced representative. Translation by a relation-lattice vector preserves the represented class and acceptance event, apart from box boundaries. This yields conditional distribution control.

**[P]** A single primitive NFS row cannot be quasi-uniform on \(\Lambda/2\): its support has size at most the pair-box size \(L^{O(1)}\), whereas the target space can have \(2^{\Theta(D)}\) elements. Its rational component also need not lie in \(\Lambda/2\).

**[H]** This counting argument does not rule out a cheap random-exponent construction using compact power products or many rows. No such construction retaining the NFS-sized rational and algebraic values is known here. **[C]** The missing addition is a sampler with sufficient random exponent lifts *after conditioning on its acceptance transcript*, together with the required norm and runtime bounds.

**[P] Route (v): multiple fields intersect obstruction spaces.**
\[
\left(\sum_iW_i\right)^\perp=\bigcap_iW_i^\perp.
\]
Using \(W_i\cap U\) gives a sufficient construction entirely inside \(U\).

**[F]** Independence of fields alone forces rapid saturation: every field might share the same hidden obstruction space. **[C]** A sufficient addition is uniform multiplicative contraction of the surviving dual dimension, above an \(L^{o(1)}\) floor, by a factor \(1-L^{-o(1)}\) per field. Then \(L^{o(1)}\) fields suffice. Fixed-functional survival bounds can instead require \(\Theta(D)\) fields, affecting the cost.

**[P] There is a limited fixed-test barrier.** Suppose field averaging alone is uniform over \(M\le N^C\) choices. Every nonempty field event has probability at least \(1/M\). A uniform nonzero error bound therefore cannot reach the \(2^{-D}\) scale needed for a naive union bound over \(2^D\) tests, since \(D\gg\log N\).

**[F]** This proves that field averaging cannot establish RK. It proves only that this particular union-bound template fails. Structural classification, aggregate counts, exact vanishing for most tests, or additional relation-sampling randomness can evade it.

**[P]** An explicit abstract family demonstrates the gap. For even \(D\), choose \(M\) pairwise disjoint \(D/2\)-dimensional subspaces \(E_A\subset\mathbf F_2^D\), using a finite-field spread. Sample rows uniformly from \(E_A^\perp\). Every fixed nonzero functional is an obstruction for at most one field, yet every field has deficiency \(D/2\).

Likewise, rows \((e_i,e_i)\) have fully covered rational coordinates but \(W=0\). Repetition makes coverage arbitrarily large. These are counterexamples to proposed probabilistic deductions, **not arithmetic counterexamples to LV’s law**.

**[H]** I found no explicit family satisfying the intended LV supply and box conditions that disproves RK. The cubic experiment [E3’s implementation](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/src/hypD/rank.py) tests raw matrix membership and supplies no genuine-certificate asymptotic theorem.

**[P] The relaxed cost formula is correct with a supply condition.** Define \(\theta\) through a certified fruitful-row cost \(L[1/2,\theta/(2a)]\), and put \(\eta=3/(d+1)\). For \(0\le\kappa\le1\), deficiency \(D^\kappa\) requires \(D^\kappa+1\) target rows. The three cost constants are
\[
2a,\qquad a+\frac{\eta}{2a},\qquad
\kappa a+\frac{\theta}{2a}.
\]
Thus
\[
\boxed{c_{\min}
=\sqrt{\,2\max\{\eta,\theta/(2-\kappa)\}\,}.}
\]
Your expression
\[
\boxed{c=\sqrt{\frac{2\theta}{2-\kappa}}}
\]
holds when \(\eta\le\theta/(2-\kappa)\). For Vallée’s \(\theta=2/3\), fixed odd \(d\ge9\), this holds throughout \(0\le\kappa\le1\). It beats \(c=1\) exactly when
\[
\kappa<2/3.
\]

**[C]** These remain conditional factoring bounds: supply, rank, and efficient certificate processing must hold together under the field law actually reached by the algorithm. Separate positive-probability assertions about unrelated field subsets do not suffice.

The literature supports the following distinctions:

- **[C] Stange 2022/2023.** Hypothesis 3.1 concerns saturation on one coordinate axis of an integer relation lattice, rather than generation of the entire lattice. The extracted exponents are always multiples of the relevant order; recovering the order with the asserted probability is conditional. NFS adaptation is suggested, not proved for its kept law. **[P]** The printed coprimality probability \(1-1/\zeta(c+1)\) is reversed: it should be \(1/\zeta(c+1)\). [Primary text](https://arxiv.org/html/2211.06821v2).

- **[C] Hafner–McCurley 1989.** Their imaginary-quadratic class-group computation and relation-generation analysis are rigorous under ERH, with constant \(\sqrt2\). **[H]** Buchmann 1990 extends the framework to general fields using additional distributional heuristics; it is not an unconditional general-field generation theorem.

- **[C/P] Seysen–Lenstra and Lenstra–Pomerance.** Seysen’s relation algorithm and A. K. Lenstra’s improvement use GRH. Lenstra–Pomerance remove it with multipliers and prove unconditional \(L[1/2,1]\) factoring. Their linear algebra needs any dependency; hidden random exponent lifts control the resulting 2-torsion. They do **not** prove saturation of ordinary sieve relations. **[F]** The Schnorr–Lenstra random-class-group/SPAR heuristic belongs to the same proved lineage: LP explicitly refute its assumed runtime behavior on a substantial family. [LP](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper85.pdf).

- **[P] Balister–Bollobás–Morris 2018.** They prove the sharp square-dependency threshold \(e^{-\gamma}J(x)\) for independent uniform integers. Below threshold this gives full row rank with high probability. It does not establish the desired NFS projected span. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1608.03857).

- **[P] Sparse random matrices.** Costello–Vu’s cited sparse symmetric result concerns complex/characteristic-zero rank, so it cannot simply be transferred to \(\mathbf F_2\). Coja-Oghlan et al. prove normalized-rank and full-row-rank results for specified random configuration models, including finite fields. NFS rows have not been shown to follow those models; normalized rank alone also falls short of RK. [Costello–Vu](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0711.2696), [normalized rank](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.05757), [full rank](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.14090).

- **[C] A relevant 2025–2026 advance.** De Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski prove general class-group/unit-group computation and log-\(S\)-unit lattice generation under ERH. Their distribution control and generation theorem are relevant precedents. Their sampler uses general algebraic elements and Arakelov randomization, rather than the restricted \(a-b\alpha\) family. [Preprint, §§17–18](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.01588).

**[H, literature assessment]** I found no published saturation theorem for the actual integer NFS/QS kept relation law, and no unconditional classical worst-case-input factoring improvement below Lenstra–Pomerance’s \(c=1\), through 19 October 2026.

**Verdict. [P]** Raw and genuine-certificate RK differ existentially by only \(O(\log N)\) dimensions. Rational smooth numbers satisfy the desired dimension-dependent escape bound. **[C]** The best precise reduction is adaptive dual-space expansion for the joint kept law on fields reached at the supply budget. **[H]** The most promising next step is a bounded-congestion switching lemma for that joint law, initially allowing deficiency \(D^{2/3-\varepsilon}\): that weaker result would already suffice to beat \(c=1\).Both constants follow, with Design V retaining the additional hypotheses in the notes. The main correction is that rigorous **batch** smoothness testing suffices; a rigorous \(L^{o(1)}\) test for each isolated candidate is unnecessary. Throughout, algorithms are classical, \(L=\exp\sqrt{\log N\log\log N}\), \(B=L^a\), and \(h=N^{\theta+o(1)}\), with fixed \(\theta>0\).

**Task 1: the consequences are proved implications.**

The smoothness assumption gives acceptance probability
\[
p_B\ge L^{-\theta/(2a)-o(1)},
\]
because \(\log\rho(\log h/\log B)=-(\theta/(2a)+o(1))\log L\).

Dixon needs \(L^{a+o(1)}\) accepted rows. Relation collection and sparse linear algebra therefore cost
\[
L^{a+\theta/(2a)+o(1)},\qquad L^{2a+o(1)}.
\]
Minimizing their maximum gives
\[
a=\sqrt{\theta/2},\qquad \boxed{c_D=\sqrt{2\theta}}.
\]
No rank hypothesis is needed: more rows than columns force a dependency.

For fruitfulness, handle nonunits by gcd and perfect powers separately. Put \(q=|\mu_2|=2^{\omega(N)}\), so \(q\ge4\). Conditional on its square, every root has probability at least \(1/(\kappa q)\). Fix all other roots in a dependency containing a sampler row. Exactly two choices are bad, hence
\[
\Pr(\text{proper factor})\ge\frac{q-2}{\kappa q}\ge\frac1{2\kappa}.
\]
Dependency selection must inspect squares and certificates only. This costs \(L^{o(1)}\) repetitions; there is no \(\kappa^{\#\text{rows}}\) loss.

For Design V, write \(\delta=3/(d+1)\). Under the **unproved LV supply transfer, genuine-certificate RK, and effective LV §6 postprocessing preserving a nonzero sampler projection**, the three exponents are
\[
2a,\qquad a+\frac{\delta}{2a},\qquad\frac{\theta}{2a}.
\]
RK deficiency \(r=L^{o(1)}\) requires a batch of \(r+1\) sampler rows, rather than a claim that one target individually lands in the span. Taking \(a=\sqrt\theta/2\) gives
\[
\boxed{c_V=\sqrt\theta},\qquad
\boxed{\delta\le\theta/2}.
\]
Thus \(d+1\ge6/\theta\), with \(d\) fixed and odd. For \(\theta=2/3\), \(d\ge9\) suffices. Raw matrix membership alone is insufficient, as [hypD_results.md §5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/hypD_results.md:545) correctly specifies.

If each sampler call costs \(L^{s+o(1)}\), the proved optimization becomes
\[
\boxed{c_D(s)=s+\sqrt{2\theta}},
\]
and, conditionally for Design V,
\[
c_V(s)=\min_{a>0}\max\left\{2a,\ a+\frac{\delta}{2a},\
s+\frac{\theta}{2a}\right\}.
\]
When the LV term does not bind,
\[
\boxed{c_V(s)=\frac{s+\sqrt{s^2+4\theta}}2},
\quad \delta\le c_V(s)^2/2.
\]
Consequently Dixon beats \(1\) when \(s+\sqrt{2\theta}<1\); Design V does so when \(s+\theta<1\), subject to its hypotheses.

The hidden losses can be controlled rigorously:

- Choose an explicit preprocessing cutoff \(y_0=\exp\sqrt{\log N}\). Trial division costs \(L^{o(1)}\), and afterwards \(\omega(N)\le\sqrt{\log N}\), giving \(2^\omega=L^{o(1)}\). A vaguely specified \(L^{o(1)}\) cutoff alone would not establish this.
- Vallée’s original counting theorem assumes prime factors exceeding \(L\). Pollard–Strassen removes factors up to \(L\) in \(L^{1/2+o(1)}\), within both constants at \(\theta=2/3\). A hypothetical smaller-\(\theta\) theorem should not inherit that stronger cutoff unnecessarily.
- Bernstein’s deterministic batch algorithm identifies smooth parts, and his related algorithms recover the factorizations, in time essentially linear in the total input length. Batches of at least \(B\) candidates make the amortized cost \(L^{o(1)}\). Such batches fit both optimizations. Thus no ECM smoothness hypothesis is required here. [D. J. Bernstein, *How to find smooth parts of integers*, 10 May 2004, Theorems 2.2–2.3](https://nymity.ch/anomalous-tor-keys/bibliography/pdf/Bernstein04.pdf).

**Smoothness counting in square classes does not introduce a fixed extra constant.** Here is the product argument, including multiplicity.

Set \(Y=\sqrt h\). Standard smooth-number estimates, with a weighted Rankin bound discarding excessive numbers of prime factors, give a set \(S\) of coprime \(B\)-smooth integers at most \(Y\) such that
\[
|S|=Y L^{-\theta/(4a)+o(1)},\qquad
\Omega(u)\le K=\sqrt{\log N}(\log\log N)^{1/4}=o(\log L).
\]
Excluding primes dividing the preprocessed \(N\) preserves this estimate. The underlying smooth-number lower bound is [Canfield–Erdős–Pomerance, *J. Number Theory* **17** (1983), 1–28](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper39.pdf).

Partition \(S\) into its \(q\) square classes modulo \(N\). At least \(|S|^2/q\) ordered pairs have the same class; their products are squares modulo \(N\), smooth, and at most \(h\). Each product has at most
\[
\tau(uv)\le2^{2K}=L^{o(1)}
\]
representations. Hence there are at least
\[
hL^{-\theta/(2a)-o(1)}
\]
distinct such products. This proves the required lower bound under quasi-uniform sampling over the interval’s square classes. Using merely the unrestricted bound \(\tau(n)=N^{o(1)}\) would be too weak. **Fibre symmetry by itself supplies no smoothness law**; that remains a separate sampler requirement.

**Task 2: \(2/3\) is Vallée’s curvature–density threshold.**

Writing \(x=x_1+t\), the exact condition is
\[
s\equiv x_1^2+2x_1t\pmod N,\qquad |s+t^2|\le h.
\]
The affine lattice has determinant \(N\). On a tangent cell \(|t|\le T\), absorbing the quadratic remainder within width \(O(h)\) requires \(T=O(\sqrt h)\). Its largest useful area is therefore
\[
Th=O(h^{3/2}).
\]
The determinant scale is reached when \(h^{3/2}\gtrsim N\), namely \(\theta\ge2/3\).

Vallée’s actual proof retains the two parabolas and uses variable Farey intervals, rather than assuming every tangent rectangle behaves regularly. Near \(aN/(2q)\), a basis includes
\[
(q,\,2qx_1-aN);
\]
lattice points lie on nearly horizontal lines with horizontal spacing \(q\) and transverse spacing \(N/q\). Continued fractions locate the interval. Line counts in the “legs,” and exact counts on the bounded number of exceptional “chest/feet” lines, give sampling weights.

With Farey order \(K\asymp N/h\), the largest denominators reach the curvature scale precisely when \(K\lesssim\sqrt h\). Below it, the uniform lower counts fail. Vallée explicitly discusses this failure in §5.1. [B. Vallée, *Generation of elements with small modular squares and provably fast integer factoring algorithms*, **Math. Comp. 56** (1991), 823–849, Theorem 5 and §5.1](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/253163661_Generation_of_elements_with_small_modular_squares_and_provably_fast_integer_factoring_algorithms).

**Task 3(a): the proposed rank-one collapse is proved, with a specific scope.**

For \(x=x_1+\sum_iw_it_i\), the quadratic remainder is
\[
Q(\mathbf t)=\left(\sum_iw_it_i\right)^2,
\]
whose matrix \(ww^\mathsf T\) has rank one. If \(Q(\mathbf t)\le Ch\) as an ordinary integer throughout the parameter domain, the distinct increments lie in an interval of length \(O(\sqrt h)\). Extra parameters supply multiplicities, not extra distinct \(x\)’s.

On a symmetric box, this follows directly from
\[
\max_{\lvert t_i\rvert\le T_i}|w\cdot t|
=\sum_i|w_i|T_i.
\]
Thus the claimed higher-dimensional volume gain is **false for this construction**. Requiring only modular smallness of \(Q\) restores the original nonlinear search problem. CRT-separated nonunit coefficients can evade the integer argument, but their gcds already expose factors.

**Task 3(b): a natural Coppersmith construction fails; a universal barrier is unproved.**

Let
\[
F(t,z)=(x_1+t)^2-z,\qquad |t|\le T,\quad |z|\le h.
\]
Consider the weighted triangular Howgrave-Graham lattice generated by
\[
N^{m-j}t^iF(t,z)^j,\qquad 0\le j\le m,\quad 0\le i\le2(m-j),
\]
with coefficient scaling \(t\mapsto Tt,\ z\mapsto hz\). Triangularity gives, exactly,
\[
n=(m+1)^2,\qquad \det\Lambda=(NT)^A h^C,
\]
\[
A=\frac{m(m+1)(4m+5)}6,\qquad
C=\frac{m(m+1)(2m+1)}6.
\]
Since \(A+C=mn\), the determinant-based short-vector condition, allowing a fixed margin for LLL and evaluation factors, approaches
\[
\boxed{T^2h<N}.
\]
But unbiased productive cells require \(Th\ge N^{1-o(1)}\). These regimes do not overlap for polynomially growing \(T\). This is a **proved failure of this shift construction**, not a necessary condition for every possible lattice.

If \(z\) is specified, univariate Coppersmith applies below \(T=N^{1/2}\), but enumerating \(N^\theta\) possible \(z\)’s is too expensive. Introducing the quotient in
\[
(x_1+t)^2-kN-z=0
\]
creates a third unknown; it does not turn the problem into the proved bivariate integer case. Generic multivariate strategies additionally need algebraic-independence assumptions.

References: Coppersmith, *J. Cryptology* **10** (1997), 233–260, [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1007/s001459900030); Howgrave-Graham, *Cryptography and Coding*, LNCS **1355** (1997), 131–142, [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0024458); Jochemsz–May, ASIACRYPT 2006, LNCS **4284**, 267–282, whose [paper explicitly describes a heuristic multivariate strategy](https://www.iacr.org/archive/asiacrypt2006/42840270/42840270.pdf).

**Task 3(c): Elkies does not provide the missing sampler.**

For \(x^2-kN\) small, tangent subdivision reproduces the curvature restriction. Scanning \(0\le x<N\) requires about \(N/\sqrt h\) tangent cells. There are at most \(2^\omega O(h)\) unit solutions, so scanning all cells costs at least \(N/h^{3/2}\), up to subexponential factors, per output.

Elkies proves a compact cover by points and line segments; general output-sensitive enumeration estimates involve heuristics. Neither yields the required quasi-uniform sampler in time subexponential in \(\log N\) below this threshold. [N. D. Elkies, *Rational points near curves and small nonzero \(|x^3-y^2|\) via lattice reduction*, ANTS IV, LNCS **1838** (2000), 33–63](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0005139).

**Task 3(d): ratios preserve the problem rather than solve it.**

If \(x^2\equiv u/v\), then
\[
(vx)^2\equiv uv,\qquad |uv|\le N^\theta.
\]
For invertible \(v\), a root-independent conversion preserves fibre symmetry.

However, starting from uniform \(x\), there are at most \(O(H^2)\) ratios with \(|u|,|v|\le H=N^{\theta/2}\), out of \(\varphi(N)/2^\omega\) square classes. Acceptance is at most
\[
N^{\theta-1+o(1)}.
\]
Conversely, choosing small \(u,v\) first leaves modular square-root extraction unresolved. This verifies the restricted counting obstruction in [nfsfruit_results.md §5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nfsfruit_results.md:324), without extending it to samplers supported on specially chosen fibres.

**Task 3(e): LP has a precise “effective exponent \(1/2\)” interpretation, but is not a small-square sampler.**

A reduced positive definite form of discriminant \(\Delta<0\) has leading coefficient
\[
A\le\sqrt{|\Delta|/3}.
\]
Random-exponent relation generation records a known word in class-group generators and reduces it to such a form. Smoothness of \(A\) produces a relation. With \(|\Delta|=N^{1+o(1)}\), this is the size-\(N^{1/2}\) analogue of relation sampling; the smoothness exponent is \(1/(4a)\). The comparison explains the constant \(1\), but the certificates and extraction mechanism are class-group relations and ambiguous forms, not individual modular-root fibres.

LP removes GRH using discriminant multipliers and character estimates. Hafner–McCurley’s class-group analysis assumes ERH. Schnorr–Lenstra’s **random class groups** method, depending on class-number smoothness, must be distinguished from the Seysen relation method. Exact references: [Seysen, *Math. Comp.* **48** (1987), 757–780](https://scispace.com/pdf/a-probabilistic-factorization-algorithm-with-quadratic-forms-4sxy4xmvw9.pdf); [Hafner–McCurley, *JAMS* **2** (1989), 837–850](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0894-0347-1989-1002631-0); [Lenstra–Pomerance, *A rigorous time bound for factoring integers*, *JAMS* **5** (1992), 483–516](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper85.pdf).

There is a **proved coverage obstruction** to a smaller exponent for globally uniform class sampling. For fundamental negative \(\Delta\), only
\[
O(A\log A)
\]
classes have an integral ideal representative of norm at most \(A\), whereas
\[
h(\Delta)=|\Delta|^{1/2+o(1)}.
\]
Thus norm \(|\Delta|^\theta\), \(\theta<1/2\), covers only a polynomially small fraction of classes. Restricting to special classes avoids this count, but then relation generation and saturation require new proofs. No such factoring improvement emerged from the literature search.

**Task 3(f): polynomially small values alone are insufficient.**

A family \(x=P(t)\), \(P(t)^2\equiv g(t)\pmod N\), must establish both smoothness density and fibre coverage. Neither follows from small \(g(t)\). For nonsquare polynomials, the necessary smooth-value estimates at these parameters are generally missing.

If \(g(t)=Q(t)^2\) identically and the sampler satisfies fibre symmetry, comparison with the known root \(Q(t)\) factors \(N\) with probability at least \(1/(2\kappa)\), on unit outputs. Thus an easy square identity cannot conceal the hard part. Likewise, \(x=\lceil\sqrt N\rceil\) gives a polynomial-time size-\(O(\sqrt N)\) square, but **fails the sampler definition**.

**Task 3(g): other approaches have identifiable missing inputs.**

- **Schnorr CVP/SVP factoring:** short logarithmic approximation does not establish enough smooth residuals, useful matrix rank, or fibre symmetry. The claimed fast factoring bounds are not established rigorous improvements. See Schnorr, [*Fast Factoring Integers by SVP Algorithms, corrected*, ePrint 2021/933](https://eprint.iacr.org/2021/933).
- **Ajtai-type sieving:** the proved \(2^{O(n)}\) SVP algorithm supplies a lattice subroutine, not a distribution theorem for suitable factoring relations. [Ajtai–Kumar–Sivakumar, STOC 2001, 601–610](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=f583f933f10229081db118e7e51e2e6e505ea3c8&repid=rep1&type=pdf).
- **Boneh CRT decoding:** finds short-interval integers satisfying sufficiently many prescribed congruences. It does not prove the required supply of smooth quadratic values or root symmetry. [*Finding smooth integers in short intervals using CRT decoding*, *JCSS* **64** (2002), 768–784](https://crypto.stanford.edu/~dabo/pubs/abstracts/CRTdecode.html).
- **Coron/Cohn–Heninger:** Coron’s proved bivariate integer-root result does not remove the unknown quotient above. Multivariate approximate-common-divisor methods require approximate multiples as input and, generally, independence heuristics; those multiples are absent here. [Coron, EUROCRYPT 2004, 492–505](https://www.iacr.org/cryptodb/data/paper.php?pubkey=2177); [Cohn–Heninger, ANTS X, *Open Book Series* **1** (2013), 271–293](https://doi.org/10.2140/obs.2013.1.271).
- **Hittmeir/Harvey:** their rigorous deterministic improvements remain polynomial in \(N\), hence asymptotically slower than \(L[1/2,1]\). See [Harvey–Hittmeir, *Math. Comp.* **91** (2022), 1367–1379](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.11105), and their [2026 large-order algorithm](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11131).

**Task 4: a restricted \(2/3\) barrier is provable.**

Consider interval cells of length at most \(\sqrt h\,L^{o(1)}\), selected by a uniform ambient point with distortion at most \(L^{o(1)}\). There are at most \(2^\omega O(h)\) unit roots with \(|x^2\bmod N|\le h\). Therefore
\[
\Pr(\text{chosen cell contains a root})
\le L^{o(1)}\,\frac{h^{3/2}}N.
\]
For fixed \(\theta<2/3\), rejection needs polynomially many attempts in \(N\). This is a genuine lower bound for that class, even if local enumeration is free.

It does **not** cover arithmetic selection of productive cells, larger exact curved regions, or arbitrary polynomial-shift lattices. Area below determinant alone proves no computational lower bound.

Your sampler with \(\theta<1/2\) implies \(c<1\) by the proved Dixon reduction. It contradicts no known theorem. No universal positive lower bound on \(\theta\) can presently be unconditional: polynomial-time factoring would let one sample uniformly from \(\mu_2\), with square \(1\), giving exponent zero.

**Task 5: the substantive post-1991 sampler improvement is uniformity, not exponent.**

Coron obtained sufficiently uniform sampling for wider intervals: [*Security proof for partial-domain hash signature schemes*, CRYPTO 2002, LNCS **2442**, 613–626](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-45708-9_39). Gentry subsequently obtained **exactly uniform**, expected-polynomial-time sampling at width \(8N^{2/3}\), using computable sampling probabilities and rejection: [*How to compress Rabin ciphertexts and signatures (and more)*, CRYPTO 2004, LNCS **3152**, 179–200, §4](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-28628-8_11).

Recent relevant progress includes de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski, [*Rigorous methods for computational number theory*, arXiv:2512.01588, revised 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.01588): ERH-conditional sampling of smooth/near-prime ideals in prescribed classes. It does not supply the modular-root sampler here.

**Verdict:** The search through 19 October 2026 found no rigorous sampler satisfying your definition with fixed \(\theta<2/3\), and no unconditional classical general-purpose factoring bound \(L[1/2,c]\) with \(c<1\). The strongest justified \(2/3\) barrier here is the stated short-cell rejection bound; extending it to arbitrary lattice samplers remains unproved.
